                { stageId: "calc-s7d", topic: "Common Derivatives", module: "Practice", component: "TaxonomyMatrix", skin: "Code", config: { data: { buckets: ["Derivative is itself (or similar)", "Derivative is different form"], items: [{ id: "dr1", content: "d/dx[eˣ] = eˣ" }, { id: "dr2", content: "d/dx[sin x] = cos x" }, { id: "dr3", content: "d/dx[ln x] = 1/x" }, { id: "dr4", content: "d/dx[cos x] = -sin x" }, { id: "dr5", content: "d/dx[eˣ · constant]" }, { id: "dr6", content: "d/dx[x³] = 3x²" }], correctAssignments: { "dr1": "Derivative is itself (or similar)", "dr2": "Derivative is different form", "dr3": "Derivative is different form", "dr4": "Derivative is different form", "dr5": "Derivative is itself (or similar)", "dr6": "Derivative is different form" } }, initialState: {} }, validation: { type: "exact", condition: {} }, feedback: { success: "Derivative types classified!", error: "eˣ is special — it's its own derivative.", hint: "eˣ stays as eˣ; everything else changes form." } },
                { stageId: "calc-s7e", topic: "Negative & Fractional Powers", module: "Practice", component: "MultipleChoice", skin: "Code", config: { data: { question: "d/dx[1/x²] = d/dx[x⁻²] = ?", options: [{ id: "a", text: "-2/x³" }, { id: "b", text: "2/x³" }, { id: "c", text: "-2x⁻¹" }, { id: "d", text: "1/x³" }], correctId: "a" }, initialState: {} }, validation: { type: "exact", condition: {} }, feedback: { success: "Correct! -2x⁻³ = -2/x³.", error: "Rewrite as x⁻² first, then apply the power rule.", hint: "d/dx[x⁻²] = -2·x⁻³." } },
                { stageId: "calc-s7f", topic: "Teach: Power Rule", module: "Assessment", component: "FeynmanPrompt", skin: "Code", config: { data: { prompt: "Why does the power rule work? Try to explain the intuition behind d/dx[xⁿ] = nxⁿ⁻¹ without just stating it as a formula.", sampleAnswer: "When you expand (x+h)ⁿ using the binomial theorem, you get xⁿ + n·xⁿ⁻¹·h + (terms with h² and higher). When you compute [f(x+h) - f(x)]/h, the xⁿ cancels, and dividing by h gives n·xⁿ⁻¹ + terms with h. As h→0, all terms with h vanish, leaving just n·xⁿ⁻¹. The coefficient n comes from the binomial expansion — there are n ways that one factor of h can appear from the n copies of (x+h).", fixedFeedback: "Brilliant explanation! You connected the power rule to the binomial theorem and the definition of the derivative. The insight that 'n comes from the n ways to choose one h' is deep understanding. This is exactly the kind of reasoning that separates memorization from true comprehension!" }, initialState: {} }, validation: { type: "exact", condition: {} }, feedback: { success: "Wonderful teaching!", error: "Think about the binomial expansion of (x+h)ⁿ.", hint: "(x+h)ⁿ = xⁿ + nxⁿ⁻¹h + ... by the binomial theorem." } }
