                { stageId: "calc-s8f", topic: "Teach: Product Rule", module: "Assessment", component: "FeynmanPrompt", skin: "Code", config: { data: { prompt: "Explain why d/dx[f·g] ≠ f'·g'. What goes wrong if you just multiply the derivatives?", sampleAnswer: "When both f and g are changing simultaneously, you can't just multiply their rates of change. Think of a rectangle with width f(x) and height g(x). When x changes, the area changes because both width and height change. The total area change ≈ (new width)·Δheight + (new height)·Δwidth = f·g' + f'·g. If you used f'·g', you'd only account for a tiny rectangle of change, missing the main contributions.", fixedFeedback: "Brilliant geometric intuition! The rectangle area analogy perfectly explains why the product rule has two terms. The area of change comes from two strips (f·Δg and g·Δf), not just the corner piece (Δf·Δg). This visual reasoning is how mathematicians develop intuition. Excellent!" }, initialState: {} }, validation: { type: "exact", condition: {} }, feedback: { success: "Great teaching!", error: "Think about how a rectangle's area changes when both sides change.", hint: "Area = width × height. If both change, the area change has multiple parts." } }
